<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>Lumi</displayName>
  <description>Organization policy for the Lumi coding agent.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="Lumi">Lumi</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_Lumi">Lumi with organization policy support</string>
      <string id="Policy">Organization policy</string>
      <string id="Policy_Help">The organization policy as a lumi.policy/v1 JSON document: settings to lock (for example privacy.secret_scan or security.cli_adapters), allowed permission modes, allowed and blocked models, files the agent must never read, shell rules, and MCP server and capability pack allowlists.

Lumi applies it when it starts. Locked settings show "Managed by" and the organization name in Settings, and people can't change them. If the document is invalid, Lumi refuses model requests until it is fixed.

See docs/enterprise-policy.md in the Lumi repository for the full format.</string>
      <string id="PolicyFile">Organization policy file</string>
      <string id="PolicyFile_Help">A path to a lumi.policy/v1 JSON file, for example on a managed share or in %ProgramData%. Only machine folders expand, from Windows: %ProgramData%, %ALLUSERSPROFILE%, %ProgramFiles%, %SystemRoot%, %windir% and %SystemDrive%. Other variables, which each user can set, stay as written. Ignored when "Organization policy" is also set.

The file and every folder above it must be owned by Administrators, SYSTEM or TrustedInstaller (for a UNC path to a file server also this computer's domain's Domain Admins or Enterprise Admins), and nobody else may change them. Name a share by its UNC path, not a mapped drive letter. A folder created under %ProgramData% lets every user add files until you lock it down (see docs/enterprise-policy.md). If the file can't be read, for example because the share is out of reach, or others can change it, Lumi refuses model requests until that's fixed; it never falls back to another policy.</string>
      <string id="PolicyKeys">Trusted policy signing keys</string>
      <string id="PolicyKeys_Help">A JSON object mapping key ids to base64 Ed25519 public keys, for example {"acme-2026": "MCowBQ..."}. Lumi accepts a signed policy, and a Lumi Cloud policy, only when it verifies against one of these keys or the machine policy's trusted_keys. On Windows this is the only place for them: a policy-keys.json file isn't read.</string>
      <string id="LicenseKeys">Trusted license signing keys</string>
      <string id="LicenseKeys_Help">A JSON object mapping key ids to base64 Ed25519 public keys that sign Lumi offline licenses, for example {"luminary-2026": "MCowBQ..."}. Lumi shows a license (lumi license status, Settings &gt; Offline mode) only when it verifies against one of these keys or a key built into Lumi. See docs/offline.md.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="Policy">
        <multiTextBox refId="Policy">Policy document (JSON):</multiTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="PolicyFile">
        <textBox refId="PolicyFile">
          <label>Path to policy.json:</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="PolicyKeys">
        <textBox refId="PolicyKeys">
          <label>Trusted keys (JSON):</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="LicenseKeys">
        <textBox refId="LicenseKeys">
          <label>Trusted license keys (JSON):</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
